comments.text:
This bug has been discussed further at <https://github.com/r-devel/r-dev-day/issues/47>, and George Stagg and I have taken this up as part of the R Dev Day @ SIP.

Picking up on the suggestion there to explore narrow rectangles, with print debugging, we found that in `cairoRectPath`, when calling 

```
png(width=100, height=100, type="cairo")
grid.rect(x=.01, width=.005, just="left", gp=gpar(col=NA, fill="black"))
dev.off()
```

we saw the debug output `1.0, 1.5`, and no rectangle was drawn.

Noting that we *do* get a rectangle drawn when switching to `type="quartz"`, we added the same print debugging statement to `QuartzRectPath`

```
static void QuartzRectPath(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1,
                           CGContextRef ctx)
{
    printf("%f, %f\n", x0, x1);
    CGContextAddRect(ctx, CGRectMake(x0, y0, x1 - x0, y1 - y0));
}
```

Then running 

```
png(width=100, height=100, type="quartz")
grid.rect(x=.01, width=.005, just="left", gp=gpar(col=NA, fill="black"))
dev.off()
```

We saw print debug output `1.0, 2.0` and a rectangle was drawn.

From there, we ran a backtrace and found code in `RQuartz_Rect` that has been specifically added to solve an issue with `image()`:

```
static void RQuartz_Rect(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1, CTXDESC)
{
    DRAWSPEC;
    if (!ctx) NOCTX;
    if (xd->flags & QDFLAG_RASTERIZED) {
        /* in the case of borderless rectangles snap them to pixels.
           this solves issues with image() without introducing other artifacts.
           other approaches (disabling anti-aliasing, drawing background first,
           snapping rect with borders) don't work as well, because they have
           unwanted visual side-effects. */
        if (R_ALPHA(gc->fill) > 0 && R_ALPHA(gc->col) == 0) {
	    /* store original values in case we need to go back */
	    double ox0 = x0, ox1 = x1, oy0 = y0, oy1 = y1;
            x0 = (round(x0 * xd->scalex)) / xd->scalex;
            x1 = (round(x1 * xd->scalex)) / xd->scalex;
            y0 = (round(y0 * xd->scaley)) / xd->scaley;
            y1 = (round(y1 * xd->scaley)) / xd->scaley;
	    /* work-around for PR#13744 - make sure the width or height
	       does not drop to 0 because of aligning. */
	    if (x0 == x1 && (ox0 != ox1)) x1 += ox1 - ox0;
	    if (y0 == y1 && (oy0 != oy1)) y1 += oy1 - oy0;
        }
    }

...
```

We took a similar approach to solving the `image()` issue above with Cairo (we've removed the scaling -- it looked to us like it was handled elsewhere -- do we need to add anything back?):

```
static void cairoRect(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1,
                      const pGEcontext gc, pDevDesc dd, int op) 
{
    pX11Desc xd = (pX11Desc) dd->deviceSpecific;

	/* solve issue with image(), similar to approach taken in RQuartz_Rect*/
    if (R_ALPHA(gc->fill) > 0 && R_ALPHA(gc->col) == 0) {
	    double ox0 = x0, ox1 = x1, oy0 = y0, oy1 = y1;
            x0 = round(x0);
            x1 = round(x1);
            y0 = round(y0);
            y1 = round(y1);
	    if (x0 == x1 && (ox0 != ox1)) x1 += ox1 - ox0;
	    if (y0 == y1 && (oy0 != oy1)) y1 += oy1 - oy0;
    }

	...
```

With this patch, we get a 1px rectangle in the example at the top of this comment, and it also fixes the issue in the original reproducible example (so now called ImageWithoutWriteStripe.png):

```
png("ImageWithoutWhiteStripe.png", width = 330, height = 200, type = "cairo")
image(matrix(1, nrow = 300, ncol = 1))
dev.off()
```

This leaves us with Xlib, where the behaviour is different again. Although using Xlib solves the white stripe example, it does **not** show a rectangle in this case:

```
png(width=100, height=100, type="Xlib")
grid.rect(x=.01, width=.005, just="left", gp=gpar(col=NA, fill="black"))
dev.off()
```

We believe that this is because whilst Quartz (and cairo with the patch) *round* values, Xlib *truncates* them by casting them to `int`, during the call to `XFillRectangle` or `XDrawRectangle` within the `X11_Rect` function:

```
static void X11_Rect(double x0, double y0, double x1, double y1,
		     const pGEcontext gc, pDevDesc dd)
{
    double tmp;
    pX11Desc xd = (pX11Desc) dd->deviceSpecific;

    if (x0 > x1) {
	    tmp = x0;
	    x0 = x1;
	    x1 = tmp;
    }
    if (y0 > y1) {
	    tmp = y0;
	    y0 = y1;
	    y1 = tmp;
    }
    CheckAlpha(gc->fill, xd);
    if (R_OPAQUE(gc->fill)) {
	    SetColor(gc->fill, xd);
	    XFillRectangle(display, xd->window, xd->wgc, (int)x0, (int)y0,
		       (int)x1 - (int)x0, (int)y1 - (int)y0);
    }
    CheckAlpha(gc->col, xd);
    if (R_OPAQUE(gc->col)) {
	    SetColor(gc->col, xd);
	    SetLinetype(gc, xd);
	    XDrawRectangle(display, xd->window, xd->wgc, (int)x0, (int)y0,
		       (int)x1 - (int)x0, (int)y1 - (int)y0);
    }
}

```

We prefer the approach for Quartz to that for Xlib and are keen to know what R Core think. 

If our diagnosis of the issue is correct, we think the next steps would be:

- test/patch in the fix for cairo
- add a new test for this image() issue
- consider the X11 approach and potentially bring it in line with the Quartz approach
